Stacks on m80-tv7i: replace the host-side stat-poll for `<vsock_uds>_<port>` with an inotify watch (CREATE on the chroot dir) so the kernel wakes us the instant the companion file appears. Zero CPU between events; sub-millisecond wakeup vs the 10 ms poll bound from m80-tv7i.

## Cost comparison

| approach | wakeup latency | CPU between events |
|---|---|---|
| stat poll (m80-tv7i) | ~10 ms (poll interval) | ~1 µs/probe |
| inotify | <1 ms (kernel notify) | 0 |

## Design (one function, two phases, fallback inline)

```rust
let chroot = vsock_uds.parent().expect("chroot dir");
let companion_name = format!("{}_{port}", vsock_uds.file_name()?);
let inotify = Inotify::init()?;                       // EMFILE / unsupported -> fall back to stat poll
let _watch = inotify.add_watch(chroot, WatchMask::CREATE)?;
// TOCTOU: file may have appeared between bind() (already done) and add_watch.
if chroot.join(&companion_name).exists() { /* go */ }
loop {
    let mut buf = [0u8; 1024];
    let events = inotify.read_events_blocking_with_timeout(&mut buf, deadline_remaining)?;
    if events.iter().any(|e| e.name.as_deref() == Some(OsStr::new(&companion_name))) { break; }
}
// then one-shot Channel::open_uds_only.
```

## TOCTOU note

Window: between guestd's `VsockListener::bind` (which makes Firecracker create the companion) and our `add_watch` arming, the file may already exist. We MUST stat once after add_watch and break early on hit, otherwise we wait forever on an event that already fired.

## Linux-only

This crate path is Linux-only. m80-preflight gates the OS, so we don't need cross-platform abstractions; document the Linux requirement in the crate README.

## Acceptance

- inotify dep added to m80-vsock with rationale (raw `inotify` crate vs `notify`).
- Phase: `add_watch` then stat-once for TOCTOU; then blocking read with deadline_remaining.
- Fallback inline: if `Inotify::init` or `add_watch` fails (EMFILE, fs without inotify), fall back to m80-tv7i's stat-poll loop in the same function. No abstraction layer.
- Bench shows ready latency drop vs m80-tv7i baseline (host-side wakeup p50/p95). Honest framing: marginal vs m80-tv7i alone (~10 ms -> <1 ms); meaningful when stacked with m80-6a0q minimal-init.
- Unit test: simulate the file appearing partway through (after add_watch but before first read) — must succeed via TOCTOU stat without a spurious timeout.

## Relationship

Stacks on m80-tv7i (now an explicit blocks dep). m80-tv7i alone gets us SmolVM-class. This bead gets us strictly better, with a clean fallback to m80-tv7i if inotify is unavailable.

Add an e2e test (or test pair) under m80-firecracker/tests/ that pins the Never-mode behavior: with --writeback never, the host workspace directory is unchanged regardless of the guest's exit code.

## Acceptance

Two cells:
- Never + exit 0: guest writes /workspace/file.txt and exits 0; host workspace dir contents unchanged after VM stops.
- Never + exit != 0: guest writes /workspace/file.txt then exits 1 (or 7, etc.); host workspace dir contents unchanged.

Both cells share most of the harness; can be one parameterized test or two functions. Recommended naming: never_with_exit_0_preserves_host and never_with_exit_nonzero_preserves_host so a regression has an obvious test name.

## Test shape

\`\`\`rust
// pseudocode
let host_ws = TempDir::new().unwrap();
fs::write(host_ws.path().join("seed.txt"), b"original")?;
let host_ws_before_state = snapshot_dir(host_ws.path());

let m80 = m80_run()
    .workspace(host_ws.path())
    .writeback(WritebackMode::Never)
    .argv(["sh", "-c", "echo new > /workspace/added.txt; exit 0"])
    .run_to_completion()?;

assert_eq!(m80.exit_code, Some(0));
let host_ws_after = snapshot_dir(host_ws.path());
assert_eq!(host_ws_before_state, host_ws_after,
    "Never mode must not extract changes");
assert!(!host_ws.path().join("added.txt").exists());
\`\`\`

Then a second variant with `exit 1` in the argv.

## Gating

#[ignore = "requires real KVM"] matching the existing end_to_end_real_kvm.rs pattern.

## Sources
- Parent: m80-kd90 (e2e writeback matrix umbrella).
- 2026-05-08 conversation.

Add an e2e test (or test pair) under m80-firecracker/tests/ that pins OnSuccess-mode behavior: extracts changes back to host on guest exit 0, leaves host untouched on non-zero exit. **This is the highest-value test in the matrix** because OnSuccess is m80's headline writeback policy (the build-succeeded-keep-output, build-failed-don't-pollute case) and the gate is exactly the place a refactor could silently invert.

## Acceptance

Two cells, both required:
- **OnSuccess + exit 0**: guest writes /workspace/added.txt and exits 0; host workspace dir contains added.txt with expected contents post-stop.
- **OnSuccess + exit != 0**: guest writes /workspace/added.txt then exits 1; host workspace dir does NOT contain added.txt; pre-existing seed.txt unchanged.

Recommended names: on_success_with_exit_0_extracts and on_success_with_failed_exit_does_not_extract.

## Test shape

\`\`\`rust
// pseudocode for the failed-exit case (the more important assertion)
let host_ws = TempDir::new().unwrap();
fs::write(host_ws.path().join("seed.txt"), b"original")?;

let m80 = m80_run()
    .workspace(host_ws.path())
    .writeback(WritebackMode::OnSuccess)
    .argv(["sh", "-c", "echo new > /workspace/added.txt; exit 1"])
    .run_to_completion()?;

assert_eq!(m80.exit_code, Some(1));
assert!(!host_ws.path().join("added.txt").exists(),
    "OnSuccess + non-zero exit must not extract");
assert_eq!(fs::read(host_ws.path().join("seed.txt"))?, b"original");
\`\`\`

## Gating

#[ignore = "requires real KVM"] matching existing end_to_end_real_kvm.rs.

## Sources
- Parent: m80-kd90.
- 2026-05-08 conversation.

`crates/m80-firecracker/tests/end_to_end_real_kvm.rs:644-673` substring-scans both `console.log` and `diagnostics.jsonl` for forbidden needles including `"malformed payload"`, `"malformed frame"`, `"oversized payload"`, etc. The intent is to catch unexpected protocol-error warnings during normal operation.

The assertion is too coarse:

1. `m80-proto/src/error.rs:19` defines `ProtoError::MalformedPayload` with Display `"malformed payload: {0}"`. m80-guestd's `connection/protocol_log.rs` emits this Display string in protocol-error log messages. When the broader end_to_end_real_kvm_file_ops flow happens to provoke a benign protocol-level error, the assertion fires on a substring that's part of an error-class label, not necessarily an unexpected-warning condition.
2. console.log is the guest serial console — kernel boot output, systemd traffic, anything the guest userspace prints. Substring matches against this firehose produce false positives.
3. The dedicated `fileop_errors_real_kvm` test passes because it doesn't carry this assertion; semantic correctness of file-op error variants is already verified there.

## Refactor

Parse `diagnostics.jsonl` line-by-line as JSON, scope the assertion to entries that represent unexpected events:

```rust
fn assert_protocol_logs_are_clean(diag_path: &Path) {
    let text = std::fs::read_to_string(diag_path).unwrap_or_default();
    for (idx, line) in text.lines().enumerate() {
        let entry: serde_json::Value = match serde_json::from_str(line) {
            Ok(v) => v,
            Err(_) => continue, // non-JSON lines aren't protocol errors
        };
        let phase = entry.get("phase").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or("");
        let message = entry.get("message").and_then(|v| v.as_str()).unwrap_or("");
        // Only fail on lines whose phase signals a protocol fault, not on
        // every log mentioning a forbidden substring.
        if phase == "wire-protocol-error" || phase == "vsock-fault" {
            panic!(
                "diagnostics.jsonl line {} reports unexpected protocol fault: phase={phase} message={message}",
                idx + 1
            );
        }
    }
}
```

This requires confirming the actual phase values m80-firecracker uses for protocol faults. If the producer side doesn't tag entries with a phase that distinguishes faults from informational/expected events, that's the prerequisite work.

## Files

- `crates/m80-firecracker/tests/end_to_end_real_kvm.rs:644-673` (rewrite both `assert_run_dir_has_no_protocol_warnings` and `assert_protocol_logs_are_clean`)
- `crates/m80-observability/src/lib.rs` — verify the `phase` enum used in diagnostics records (if needed, add a stable `wire-protocol-error` phase distinct from informational events)
- console.log is dropped from this assertion entirely; it remains captured in run-dir for debugging via RunDirDumpGuard

## Acceptance

- end_to_end_real_kvm_file_ops passes
- A regression that emits a real protocol fault (e.g., a synthetic test that injects a malformed frame) still fails the assertion, with the fault's phase + message reported clearly
- A benign protocol_log entry whose Display happens to contain forbidden substrings does NOT fail the assertion
- doc at `docs/behaviors/diagnostics/protocol-log-classification.md` records the phase taxonomy
- CHANGELOG: test(real-kvm): scope protocol-warning assertion to diagnostics phase, not raw substring
